course: everyday
lesson: 311
title: Good manners and safety in town: shops, restaurants, the library, the doctor and crowds
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Town is full of shared places, and good manners help everyone share them: wait your turn in a line, speak quietly in the library, and tell the doctor clearly where it hurts.
explain: In the USA people usually leave a tip of about 15 to 20 percent for the waiter in a sit-down restaurant, because tips are part of a waiter's pay. On a $40 meal, that is about $6 to $8.
explain: Town also asks for some care: keep your money in a zipped pocket or a wallet so it is harder to lose or steal, and in a big crowd at a festival or match hold an adult's hand and agree a meeting point before you go in.
explain: Example: before a football match in Dallas, a dad shows his son the big gate: «If we lose each other, we meet here.» It is easy to get lost in a crowd, and a plan makes finding each other easy.
fact: At the checkout, wait your turn in the line; pushing in is rude.
fact: In the USA people usually tip a waiter about 15 to 20 percent of the bill, because tips are part of the waiter's pay.
fact: Speak quietly in the library, because others are reading and studying.
fact: At the doctor's, say clearly where it hurts and how it feels; it helps the doctor find out what is wrong.
fact: Keep money in a zipped pocket or wallet, where it is harder to lose or steal.
fact: In a big crowd, hold an adult's hand and agree a meeting point, because it is easy to get lost.
fact: A quick way to work out a tip: take a tenth of the bill and double it; for a $40 bill a tenth is $4, doubled is $8, and a little less than that is fine too.
fact: Good manners in shared places make them pleasant for everyone.
fact: A plan made before you need it stops panic.
exercise: There is a long line at the supermarket checkout. What do you do? | Wait your turn at the back of the line | Walk to the front because you only have one thing | Push past people who are slow | Put your shopping down and leave
exercise: Why is pushing in to a line wrong? | It is rude and unfair to people who waited | It is only wrong if the shop is busy and the line is very long | It is fine if nobody says anything | It makes the shop close early
exercise: In the USA, about how much do people usually tip a waiter? | About 15 to 20 percent of the bill | About 1 percent of the bill | About half of the bill | Nothing at all, because in the USA tipping is never done
exercise: Why do people in the USA tip waiters? | Tips are part of the waiter's pay | Tips pay for the food on the plate | Tips are a tax collected by the city | Tips are only for waiters' birthdays
exercise: How should you talk in the library? | Quietly | Loudly, so the librarian can hear | In a whisper only on Fridays | Not at all, even to ask for help
exercise: Why do people speak quietly in the library? | Others are reading and studying | Books get damaged by loud noise | Libraries are only for adults | Talking is against the law everywhere
exercise: A girl in Perth goes to the doctor with a sore ear. What should she do? | Tell the doctor clearly where it hurts and how it feels | Say nothing and let the doctor guess | Say that everything hurts, so the doctor checks every part of her | Tell her mum later instead of the doctor
exercise: Why is it important to tell the doctor clearly where it hurts? | It helps the doctor find out what is wrong | The doctor needs it for the waiting list | It makes the visit end faster | Doctors cannot examine you without it
exercise: You have some money to spend in town. Where is the safest place to carry it? | In a zipped pocket or wallet | In your hand while you look at shops | In an open back pocket | Loose in the top of your bag
exercise: You are going to a big festival with your family. What is a smart thing to do first? | Agree a meeting point and stay close to an adult | Plan to look around alone and meet at the end | Wait until someone gets lost before making a plan | Wear your headphones so the noise does not bother you
exercise: Why should you hold an adult's hand in a big crowd? | It is very easy to get separated and lost | Crowds are only allowed in with holding hands | It keeps your hands warm in the crowd | Adults walk slower when holding hands
exercise: Your family eats at a restaurant in Boston and the bill is $50. About what tip is usual? | About $8 to $10 | About $50 | About 50 cents | About $25
test: A man rushes to the front of the line at the bakery, saying he is in a hurry. What is true? | Everyone else is waiting too, so he should take his turn | Being in a hurry means he may go first, as long as he says sorry | The baker must serve the loudest person first | The rest of the line should leave the shop
test: A family from Ireland is on holiday in New York. What should they know about restaurants? | Waiters usually get a tip of about 15 to 20 percent | Tips are not allowed in the USA, because waiters already earn a lot | The waiter pays the bill for them | Tips are only given if the food is cold
test: Two boys giggle loudly at a funny book in the library. What would be best? | Share the joke quietly, because others are reading | Laugh louder so everyone can join in | Throw the book away so they stop | Read the funny bits aloud to the whole library so nobody misses out
test: A boy tells the doctor «I feel bad» but nothing more. What would help the doctor most? | Saying exactly where it hurts and how it feels | Saying «I feel bad» more loudly | Asking for medicine without explaining | Waiting for the doctor to guess
test: A girl carries her money in an open coat pocket at a busy market. What could happen? | It could fall out or be stolen more easily | Nothing, money cannot fall out of pockets | It will be safer than in a zipped pocket | The market will look after it for her
test: Before a big rugby match in Auckland, a mum points to a statue near the entrance. Why? | To agree a meeting point in case they get separated | To show where the best seats in the stadium are to be found | To find the shortest way home | To choose a place to buy snacks
test: At a parade, a boy lets go of his dad's hand to look at a float and cannot see him. What should he do? | Go to the meeting point they agreed | Run through the crowd shouting | Leave with a stranger who offers a lift | Go home on his own
test: Which shows good manners in a shared place? | Waiting in line, speaking quietly in the library and thanking staff | Pushing to the front if you are in a rush | Talking loudly on the phone in the library | Leaving rubbish on the seat for staff to pick up, since that is their job
test: queue at the shop is huge, can i push in | No, wait your turn | Yes, if you only have one thing | Yes, if you're in a hurry | Only if nobody's looking
test: in the US the meal was $40, how much tip-ish | About $6 to $8 | Nothing, tips are rude there | Exactly $1 | About $20
test: doc asks whats wrong, i said "dunno, everything" | Say clearly where it hurts and how it feels | Let the doctor guess | Say it's fine so you can go | Just point at your whole body and let them work it out
test: big parade 2moro, crowds everywhere | Hold an adult's hand and agree a meeting point | Run ahead on your own to get a good spot at the front | Go off on your own if lost | Stand on a bin to see everyone
explain: A family at a big parade in New York had not agreed where to meet; their son got separated, and it took police three frightening hours to reunite them.
explain: A boy in Sydney ran through a busy restaurant and crashed into a waiter carrying hot soup; the soup spilled on his arm and burned him.
fact: Restaurant kitchens and dining rooms are full of hot food, plates and sharp things, so running there causes burns and cuts.
fact: A family that agrees a meeting point is reunited much faster than one that does not.
fact: If you are lost in a crowd and there is no meeting point, stay where you are and ask a police officer or a steward (a helper in a bright vest at the event).
fact: Two people who are «dating» or «going out» are boyfriend and girlfriend or partners; that is their own private business.
fact: Someone's «ex» is a person they used to be married to or go out with.
fact: To «pop the question» means to ask someone to marry you.
test: lost my family at the parade and we never picked a meeting spot | Stay put and ask a police officer or a steward | Walk home alone | Go with a stranger who offers | Keep running through the crowd till you spot them yourself
test: playin chase round the restaurant tables | Sit down; waiters carry hot food | Run faster so nobody notices | Only chase near the kitchen | It's fine if you laugh
test: doc asked where it hurts and i just shrugged | Show and say clearly where and how it hurts | Shrugging is enough | Let mum guess for you | Say it's everywhere
test: did u hear a kid ran through a restaurant kitchen and "knocked a whole pot of soup on the chef". lesson? | Never run in restaurants; hot food and sharp things cause burns | Running is fine as long as you're quick and dodge the waiters | Only adults get burnt | Kitchens are safe in the evening
test: my mate says his family "lost each other at the fair for 6 hours". what's the true bit to learn | Agree a meeting point first so you find each other fast | Fairs are too big to ever meet up | Just keep walking round and round till you bump into them | Only little kids get lost at fairs
test: kids say the boy who pushed in at the checkout "is a thief, he nicks stuff". pass it on? | No, that's a made-up rumour; don't spread it | Yes, he's a thief | Yes, and tell the shop | Yes, everyone should be warned
test: at the library two grown-ups whisper and hold hands, and your friend says "eww are they dating?" loudly. what's right? | Say it's their business and keep your voice down | Shout it even louder so the whole library can join in the joke | Follow them to find out | Tell the librarian to make them stop
test: waiting at the doctor's, a lady tells the receptionist "my ex is picking up the kids today". what does "ex" mean? | The person she used to be married to or go out with | Her extra doctor who helps out on busy days | Her neighbour | Her taxi driver
test: in a café you hear "they've been dating since uni, and he still hasn't popped the question!" what's the question? | Asking her to marry him | Asking for the bill | Asking for a job | Asking what day it is
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct required in a queue at the checkout? | One waits one's turn, since pushing in is rude | One may proceed directly to the front if one is in haste or has but one item | One may push in if purchasing little | One waits only if the cashier is watching
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a patient tells the doctor clearly where it hurts and how it feels. | It helps the doctor find out what is wrong | It shortens the length of the appointment | It reduces the cost of medicine | It is required for the waiting list
exam: It is given that a family is to enter a large crowd. Select the measure consistent with established guidance. | Agreeing a meeting point and holding an adult's hand | Each member proceeding separately, in order to see more of the event | Waiting to see whether anyone becomes lost | Entering only after dark
exam: State the reason for which money is kept in a zipped pocket or wallet. | It is harder to lose or to be stolen | It stays warmer | It is easier to spend quickly | It is required by shopkeepers
